I had actually written a paragraph about the potential legal standing of 
"NetHack" as a trademark, but I deleted it before I posted.  ;-)

Partially that was because I didn't think it should be necessary, since 
I tend to think anyone who plays NetHack is probably going to be nicer 
and more polite, as well as open to being convinced by discussion, than 
a typical "gamer" would be.  It was also because I figure the DevTeam is 
unlikely to pursue any legal action against a fork project, but I could 
be wrong about that.

Trademark and other "intellectual property" cases are also notoriously 
difficult to predict, even if the issues seem completely obvious.  Now, 
I Am Not A Lawyer but here's one example adapted from what I was told by 
the IP lawyers I consulted last year when the Smurfs were threatening to 
sue me over a web site I run (at <http://websmurfer.devnull.net>):

In the U.S. at least, trademark law only applies to "commerce" which 
requires that money, goods or services change hands.  Since the DevTeam 
charges nothing for NetHack (and as far as I know never has) and I doubt 
that Jim's fork would either, this would seem pretty cut and tried: no 
commerce, no trademark.

On the other hand, third-parties _have_ distributed NetHack commercially 
in the past and you could make the argument that the fact that it's 
still a bundled component of many open source operating system 
distributions (which are sold commercially as well as being available 
for free download) should be seen to establish a commercial setting for 
the name "NetHack" and thus it should be a defensible trademark.

There's no way to predict how that would be decided by a court.  ;-)

